  <p class="lede first reveal">Ask a woman who has been circling a digital business for eight months what's holding her back and she will almost always say some version of the same thing: <em>I don't really know how to sell.</em> It sounds like the honest answer. It is almost never the true one. Because if you hand that same woman a finished product, a live shop, a month of scheduled content and a listing that already ranks, she does not freeze at the point of sale. She sells. The selling was never the wall. The wall was four steps back, and she has been standing at it so long she has stopped seeing it as a wall at all.</p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="misdiagnosis">The wrong diagnosis costs you the year</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">"I need to learn to sell" is what a stalled business sounds like from the inside. It sends you to a sales course when what you needed was inventory.</p>
  <p class="reveal">This is the part that quietly eats twelve months. You diagnose a selling problem, so you go and learn selling. You read about hooks and objections and pricing psychology. You get genuinely better at all of it. And at the end you are still sitting in front of a blank Canva page with nothing to apply any of it to.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Nothing you learned was wrong. It just wasn't the constraint. Learning to sell when you have nothing to sell is like getting very good at driving and never buying a car.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Name the actual blocker out loud before you buy the solution to a different one. If you cannot finish the sentence "the thing I would sell today is ___," your problem is not sales.</p></div>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="gates">The four gates, and why they feel like one</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">There are four separate jobs between you and your first sale. Most people are stuck at all four simultaneously, which is why it feels like one enormous impossible thing.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>No product.</strong> Designing one from a blank page is the job that eats the month. Not because it is hard — because it is open-ended, and open-ended work is the kind that gets postponed indefinitely.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>No shop.</strong> Etsy, Stan, Beacons. Forty-seven half-relevant tutorials, three abandoned account setups, still not live.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Nothing to post.</strong> The shop finally exists and opens onto an empty feed. Now it needs reels, Pins, carousels, stories — a second full-time job appearing at exactly the moment you thought you were finished.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Listings nobody clicks.</strong> Titles, tags, descriptions, mockups. This is where good products die quietly, and the death looks so much like "my product wasn't good enough" that most people conclude exactly that and stop.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Four gates. Solve one and you are still stuck. That is the entire reason this feels hopeless rather than merely slow.</p>
  <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">!</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Attacking the gates in order and running out of momentum at gate two. They are not sequential obstacles to be ground through. They are four things that need to arrive at once.</p></div>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="blank">The blank page is the most expensive thing you own</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">A thirty-page guide is roughly three weeks of your evenings. A hundred-piece reel pack is a month. That is the real price of starting from nothing.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Nobody counts this cost properly because it is paid in scattered hours rather than in one visible number. But do the arithmetic honestly. Write the guide, design the guide, build the mockups, write the listing, then make the content to launch it. You are three months in and you have one product.</p>
  <p class="reveal">And a shop with one product in it does not read as a shop. It reads as an experiment. Which affects conversion, which affects your belief in the whole thing, which affects whether product two ever gets made.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The women who get past this are almost never the ones who worked hardest at designing. They are the ones who found a way to skip the blank page entirely and spend their hours on the parts that actually compound — the brand, the positioning, the traffic.</p>
