  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="rebrand">The rebrand is the product</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Nobody is paying for your fonts. They are paying because you understood their situation well enough to package something for it.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Here is where women new to this get the value backwards. They assume the design is the asset and the positioning is decoration. It is the other way round, and it always has been.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Take the same wellness planner. One woman lists it as "Daily Wellness Planner — Printable PDF." Another rebrands it for night-shift nurses, changes four spreads to match a shift rotation, prices it higher, and writes a listing that names the exact problem of eating properly on nights. Same source file. Not remotely the same product, and not the same money.</p>
  <p class="reveal">What the second woman did took an afternoon. What she skipped took three months. That is the entire trade, and once you see it clearly the objection about exclusivity mostly dissolves — because the thing that made her version work was never in the file.</p>
