  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">06</span><div class="num">Chapter Six</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="thirty">What thirty days actually looks like</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Not a promise about money. A description of what would be sitting on your own hard drive, which is a thing I can be specific about.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Week one.</strong> You pick a lane and open the files. Ninety-two products, nine vaults. Choose the three that fit your niche, swap the colours, fonts and logo, and they are finished and on your desktop by Sunday.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Week two.</strong> Your shop exists. The training walks you through Etsy, Stan or Beacons, and your first listings go up with real, finished products behind them instead of placeholders.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Week three.</strong> You stop scrambling for content. A month of faceless reels and templates queued, so posting takes twenty minutes on a Sunday rather than your whole week.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Week four.</strong> The next drop lands. Twenty brand-new products, a fresh theme. Your shelf refills before it ever looks empty.</p>
  <p class="reveal">By day thirty: a storefront with real products in it, a content bank deep enough to post from for months, ninety-two rebrandable products nobody can take back, and a library that restocks itself on the first of every month.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>The licence survives cancellation. Join for one month, download every file, cancel the next morning — and all of it is still yours to edit, rebrand and sell permanently. You are paying for the doorway and the new work arriving each month, never renting what is already on your desktop.</p></div>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">07</span><div class="num">Chapter Seven</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="stops">The three things that stop people</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Each objection is reasonable. Each one has a plain answer.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>"It feels like cheating."</strong> Every shop you have ever bought from sells something it did not manufacture. Wholesale is not a moral category, it is how retail works. What you are adding is the brand, the audience understanding and the distribution — which is the part that was always doing the work.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>"I can't design."</strong> You do not need to. Everything opens in Canva as an editable template and the rebranding training covers the workflow. If you can change a colour and type your name, you can finish a product.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>"I'll build my own once I know what sells."</strong> You cannot know what sells until something is listed. This is the loop that keeps people at the starting line for a year — waiting for market data that only exists on the other side of publishing. Start with rebranded inventory, watch what moves, then build originals into the demand you have proven.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The gap between the women doing this and the women reading about doing this is almost never talent. It is that one group has something on the shelf and the other is still deciding what to make.</p>
